TOPIC = {'title': 'Tool Chaining, Parallel Tool Calls & Tool Tracing', 'slug': 'tool-chaining-parallel-tool-calls-tool-tracing', 'description': 'Apply tool chaining, parallel tool calls & tool tracing to design, implement, debug, and evaluate a bounded agentic system.', 'difficulty': 'Intermediate → Advanced', 'estimated_minutes': 55, 'prerequisite_ids': [], 'lesson': {'lesson_id': 'L012-016', 'content_markdown': '# L012-016 — Tool Chaining, Parallel Tool Calls & Tool Tracing\n\n## Learning objective\n\nApply **Tool Chaining, Parallel Tool Calls & Tool Tracing** to design or improve a bounded, observable agentic AI system. By the end of the lesson, you should be able to explain the core mechanism, implement the control boundary, diagnose one realistic failure mode, and define how the behavior will be evaluated.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nAgentic systems become difficult to trust when control decisions are hidden inside a large prompt or a single unrestricted agent. This lesson makes **tool chaining** and **dependencies** explicit architectural concerns. The goal is not to add agent components for their own sake; it is to make the system easier to reason about, test, debug, secure, and operate.\n\nA strong implementation should always answer four questions:\n\n1. **What state does the component read?**\n2. **What decision is it allowed to make?**\n3. **What action can it cause?**\n4. **What stops or redirects it when the result is poor?**\n\n## Core concepts\n\n- **tool chaining** — a design concern that must be made explicit, observable, testable, and bounded in an agentic system.\n- **dependencies** — a design concern that must be made explicit, observable, testable, and bounded in an agentic system.\n- **parallel calls** — a design concern that must be made explicit, observable, testable, and bounded in an agentic system.\n- **tool traces** — a design concern that must be made explicit, observable, testable, and bounded in an agentic system.\n- **retry/fallback decisions** — a design concern that must be made explicit, observable, testable, and bounded in an agentic system.\n\n## Engineering model\n\n```text\nInput → Structured State → Decision → Action → Observation → Evaluation\n```\n\nTreat the diagram as a control model, not as a mandatory framework. The exact implementation may use an agent SDK, LangGraph, a workflow engine, ordinary Python, or a service boundary. What matters is that state, authority, interfaces, and termination are visible.\n\n## Implementation pattern\n\n```python\ndef safe_tool_call(tool, *, args: dict, allowed: set[str]):\n    if tool.__name__ not in allowed:\n        raise PermissionError("Tool is not allowed for this agent.")\n    return tool(**args)\n```\n\nThe example is deliberately small. In a production implementation, add typed contracts, trace metadata, explicit error handling, and authorization around any side-effecting action.\n\n## Design guidance\n\n- Prefer **deterministic code** for deterministic rules such as schema checks, counters, permission gates, retry ceilings, and exact comparisons.\n- Use an **agent/model** where semantic judgment, decomposition, evidence interpretation, or adaptive strategy selection is actually required.\n- Keep interfaces typed and narrow. Free-form text between components should be the exception, not the default.\n- Treat tool results, retrieved documents, and external content as data. Do not automatically grant them authority over system instructions or policy.\n- Record enough trace information to reconstruct the path: selected action, tool input, tool result, state update, evaluator result, and next action.\n- Put ceilings around autonomous behavior: iterations, time, tokens/cost, retries, and high-risk actions.\n\n## Failure modes and debugging\n\nA useful debugging sequence is:\n\n1. Verify the **input/state** was correct before blaming the model.\n2. Verify the **interface/schema** accepted and preserved the intended fields.\n3. Inspect the **selected action or route**.\n4. Inspect tool or worker **results and failures**.\n5. Check whether the **evaluation/guardrail** is itself correct.\n6. Confirm that retry, replan, fallback, or escalation can actually terminate.\n\nCommon problems include excessive context, vague tool descriptions, stale state, repeated work, unbounded loops, over-broad permissions, brittle output parsing, and evaluators that incorrectly score acceptable behavior.\n\n## Evaluation checklist\n\nA good implementation of this lesson should be measurable. Track or test at least:\n\n- task success or contract validity;\n- number of model/tool/worker calls;\n- latency and token/cost impact;\n- retry or replan count;\n- correct termination behavior;\n- failure-path behavior;\n- whether the architecture is simpler than an equivalent over-agentic design.\n\n## Source mapping\n\n- **Source:** BOOK-012 — *AI Agents in Action*\n- **Chapter:** 2\n- **Sections:** 2.4.2\n- **Curriculum role:** CORE + INTEGRATION\n- **Pages:** SOURCE INFORMATION MISSING\n- **Masar policy:** source concepts are transformed into original instructional content; duplicated prerequisite material remains revision only.\n\n## Visual references\n\n- **Figure 2.9 (OPTIONAL)** — Tool trace  \n  Placeholder: `assets/course_012/m012_02/fig_2_9_tool_trace.png`\n\n## Practice\n\nComplete the two exercises stored in `EXERCISES` in this lesson seed file. For the implementation exercise, keep the first version small enough that you can trace every state transition manually.\n\n## Knowledge check\n\nComplete the three-question quiz in `QUIZ`. Explanations are included because the goal is to test architecture judgment rather than memorization.\n\n## Exit criteria\n\nYou are ready to continue when you can:\n\n- explain the lesson\'s control boundary without relying on framework-specific terminology;\n- implement a minimal typed version;\n- identify at least one unsafe or unreliable failure path;\n- state how the system stops, recovers, or escalates;\n- name the metric or test that would show whether the design is actually better.\n', 'source_references': [{'source_id': 'BOOK-012', 'book_title': 'AI Agents in Action', 'chapter': 2, 'sections': ['2.4.2'], 'pages': 'SOURCE INFORMATION MISSING', 'curriculum_role': 'CORE + INTEGRATION'}], 'visuals': [{'figure': '2.9', 'priority': 'optional', 'purpose': 'Tool trace', 'asset': 'assets/course_012/m012_02/fig_2_9_tool_trace.png', 'source_type': 'manual_source_figure', 'bundled': False}]}, 'exercises': [{'title': 'Architecture exercise — Tool Chaining, Parallel Tool Calls & Tool Tracing', 'type': 'design', 'instructions': 'Draw or write a small architecture that applies tool chaining and dependencies. Label state, decision boundaries, failure behavior, and the observable data you would trace.', 'acceptance_criteria': ['The responsible component is explicit.', 'Inputs and outputs are defined.', 'At least one failure path is specified.', 'The design explains why an agent is needed instead of deterministic code where applicable.']}, {'title': 'Debug/evaluate exercise — Tool Chaining, Parallel Tool Calls & Tool Tracing', 'type': 'debug_evaluate', 'instructions': 'Create a failure scenario involving retry/fallback decisions. Add a bounded recovery policy and define one metric or assertion that would prove the change improved the system.', 'acceptance_criteria': ['The failure is reproducible.', 'Retry/loop behavior is bounded.', 'Fallback, replan, block, or escalation is explicit.', 'A measurable success criterion is included.']}], 'quiz': {'title': 'L012-016 Knowledge Check', 'questions': [{'question': 'Which statement best captures the role of tool chaining in Tool Chaining, Parallel Tool Calls & Tool Tracing?', 'options': ['It makes tool chaining explicit and testable inside the system design.', 'It removes the need for all evaluation and boundaries.', 'It guarantees deterministic model behavior in every case.', 'It should remain implicit so the architecture stays simple.'], 'answer_index': 0, 'explanation': 'tool chaining should be explicit enough to inspect, test, and bound. It does not eliminate evaluation or make stochastic models deterministic.'}, {'question': 'A design using dependencies is adding latency and complexity without improving task success. What is the best engineering response?', 'options': ['Keep it because more agentic components are always better.', 'Measure its contribution and simplify or remove it if it does not earn its complexity.', 'Increase the model temperature.', 'Hide the extra steps from tracing.'], 'answer_index': 1, 'explanation': 'COURSE-012 treats agentic complexity as a cost that must be justified by measurable capability, reliability, or product value.'}, {'question': 'What should happen when retry/fallback decisions produces an ambiguous or unsafe outcome?', 'options': ['Continue indefinitely until the model becomes confident.', 'Ignore the signal if the final prose looks fluent.', 'Use a bounded policy such as retry, fallback, replan, block, or human escalation.', 'Delete all state and restart without recording the failure.'], 'answer_index': 2, 'explanation': 'Agentic systems need explicit terminal behavior and bounded recovery. Ambiguity should change control flow rather than produce uncontrolled loops.'}]}, 'project': {'title': 'Build an Observable Research Planning Agent', 'summary': 'Build a typed, tool-using research planner with dependent and independent calls, tracing, and failure handling.', 'deliverables': ['Strict typed output', 'At least two tools', 'At least one dependent tool chain', 'At least one independent/parallelizable call', 'One explicit failure path', 'Tracing and token/cost awareness', 'Least-capability tool permissions']}}
